<h1>Likutay Halachos — Hilchos Tefillin</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2>Halacha 1</h2>
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<p class="no-indent" style="font-style:italic; margin-bottom:16px;">The parchment of Tefillin must be from the hide of a kosher domesticated animal, wild animal, or bird, as it says: "L'ma'an…" — from a species that is permitted to your mouth, etc.</p>

<p class="no-indent" style="font-style:italic; margin-bottom:16px;">According to the Torahs "<span class="key-term">Hai man d'azeil l'minsav ittesa</span>" <span class="source">(Siman 29)</span> and "<span class="key-term">Tefillah LaChavakuk</span>" <span class="source">(Siman 19)</span>, see there.</p>

<p><span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are the <span class="bold-concept">rectification of <span class="key-term">Da'as</span></span>, for they are the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> <span class="bracket-def">[brains/intellect]</span>. And they are the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Tikkun HaKlali</span> <span class="bracket-def">[General Rectification]</span> — the rectification of the <span class="key-term">Bris</span>, for the rectification of <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> is the rectification of the <span class="key-term">Bris</span>. Therefore, the essential content of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is the remembrance of <span class="key-term">Yetzias Mitzrayim</span>, for the Exodus is the aspect of rectifying the <span class="key-term">Bris</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>. In Mitzrayim there was the blemish of the <span class="key-term">Bris</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>, as explained many times. Therefore it says of the exile in Mitzrayim: "<span class="transliteration">Va'e'evor alayich… v'at eirom v'eryah</span>" — "And I passed over you… and you were naked and bare" <span class="source">(Yechezkel 16:6–7)</span> — for they were naked, without garments, because the <span class="key-term">Tikkun HaKlali</span>, which is the rectification of garments, had not yet been achieved.</p>

<p>And it says there nearby: "<span class="transliteration">Va'e'evor alayich va'er'eich misboseses bedamayich</span>" — "And I passed over you and saw you wallowing in your blood" — for one depends on the other. She was naked and bare because she was wallowing in blood — the aspect of <span class="key-term">dam niddah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[menstrual blood]</span>, the aspect of "<span class="transliteration">Adom lilbushecha</span>" — "Red is Your garment" — which is the corruption of garments. When Hashem wished to rectify and redeem them, it says: "<span class="transliteration">Va'omar lach bedamayich chayi</span>" — "And I said to you: In your blood, live!" — the aspect of: the <span class="bold-concept">blood becomes turbid and turns to milk</span> <span class="source">(Niddah 9a)</span>, which is the aspect of life — the infant's sustenance — "<span class="transliteration">V'dei chalev izim l'lachmecha… v'chayim l'na'aroseicha</span>" <span class="source">(Mishlei 27:27)</span>. The blood is whitened through elevating the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. The <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> are life itself: "<span class="transliteration">HaChochmah techayeh</span>" — "Wisdom gives life" <span class="source">(Koheles 7:12)</span> — the opposite of the corruption of garments through stains, the aspect of <span class="key-term">dam niddah</span>, which is the side of death. As our Sages said <span class="source">(Shabbos 114a)</span>: "A <span class="key-term">talmid chacham</span> on whose garment a stain is found is deserving of death" — for stains come from the 365 sinews corresponding to the 365 negative commandments, whence the side of death derives. Therefore it says there immediately after "<span class="transliteration">bedamayich chayi</span>": "<span class="transliteration">Va'albisheich va'en'aleich</span>" — "And I clothed you and shod you" — for through the blood becoming turbid and turning to milk, the garments are rectified.</p>

<p>Therefore on the night of the Exodus one drinks <span class="bold-concept">four cups of wine</span> — in the aspect of: "merited — he becomes a head" <span class="source">(Yoma 76b)</span>, as explained there <span class="source">(Siman 29)</span>. They correspond to the <span class="bold-concept">four white garments</span> that the <span class="key-term">Kohen Gadol</span> wears on <span class="key-term">Yom HaKippurim</span>, when the blood becomes turbid and turns to milk — the aspect of the scarlet thread that whitens sins. The four white garments indicate that the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> — which are four: <span class="key-term">Chochmah</span>, <span class="key-term">Binah</span>, and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> which divides into two (<span class="key-term">Chassadim</span> and <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span>) — have been rectified. Then from <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> comes speech, which contains <span class="key-term">Chassadim</span> and <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span>, fire and water. As written there: through the <span class="key-term">Tikkun HaKlali</span> (rectification of <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>), one can speak. Corresponding to this are the <span class="bold-concept">four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span></span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — corresponding to the four white garments — for <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are the <span class="key-term">Tikkun HaKlali</span>.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Shin</span> of three heads and <span class="key-term">Shin</span> of four heads</span>. When Yisrael left Mitzrayim — from the blemish of the <span class="key-term">Bris</span> — they needed to count seven weeks corresponding to the seven clean days of a <span class="key-term">niddah</span>, for they emerged from the aspect of <span class="key-term">dam niddah</span>. They are seven corresponding to the <span class="bold-concept">seven orifices of the brain</span>, which are the aspect of the seven lamps of the <span class="key-term">Menorah</span> that must be rectified when the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> are elevated. Corresponding to this are the seven days of mourning (<span class="key-term">rachamana litzlan</span>), to draw down the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> that departed at death — for the essential death is the departure of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>, since "<span class="transliteration">HaChochmah techayeh</span>." This is the aspect of the seven days the one contaminated by a corpse must count. And this is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">seven heads</span> of the two <span class="key-term">Shins</span> (three plus four) — corresponding to the seven clean days, to rectify the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. They are divided into three and four because the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> are so divided: initially, before entering within, they are three; afterward, when they enter, <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> divides into two and they become four. Thus the seven days of mourning are also divided into three and four, for the first three days have stricter laws <span class="source">(Yoreh De'ah 393:1)</span>. Similarly, the seven days of a corpse require sprinkling on the third and seventh. And the seven clean days of a <span class="key-term">niddah</span> — the first three are stricter regarding a stain <span class="source">(ibid. 196:10)</span>. For the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> too are divided: in the first three days the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> have not yet begun to be rectified at all; afterward they have already been drawn down in those three days and are close to entering — hence afterward it is not as strict.</p>

<p>From all the above, <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Tikkun HaKlali</span> — rectification of the <span class="key-term">Bris</span>. The essential rectification of the <span class="key-term">Bris</span> is through the completeness of <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span> <span class="bracket-def">[the Holy Tongue]</span>, as our Rebbe said <span class="source">(Siman 19)</span>. Through <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, speech of <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span> is rectified, as Rashi explains: "<span class="transliteration">V'hayu l'totafos</span>" — its Targum is "<span class="transliteration">viy'hon l'shinunin</span>" — that whoever sees them speaks of the miracle. Thus through <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, speech of praise and thanks to Hashem is rectified — the completeness of <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore the essential <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are made of <span class="bold-concept">hide</span> — the parchment is of hide, the <span class="key-term">batim</span> <span class="bracket-def">[housings]</span> are of hide, and if made of gold they are invalid. For the completeness of <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span> comes through <span class="key-term">Leshon Targum</span> <span class="bracket-def">[the Aramaic tongue]</span> — by elevating the good within the <span class="key-term">Targum</span>. This is the aspect of hide, for hide is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Nogah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[the intermediate shell]</span> — a mixture of good and evil, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Targum</span> (also the aspect of <span class="key-term">Nogah</span>). Therefore <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> must be written on hide and constructed from hide — to elevate the good from <span class="key-term">Nogah</span> into <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span>, to fulfill "<span class="transliteration">V'hayu l'totafos</span>" and its Targum "<span class="transliteration">viy'hon l'shinunin</span>." They must be from a permitted species specifically, for the permitted species is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Targum</span>/<span class="key-term">Nogah</span> from which the good is elevated to <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span>. But the forbidden species are the aspect of the evil of the 70 languages that must be subdued, as explained there — "<span class="transliteration">Yimotu aleihem gechalim</span>" — "Let coals fall upon them."</p>

<p>This is why our Sages were precise in deriving the law that <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> must be from "a species <span class="bold-concept">permitted to your mouth</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">hamutar l'ficha</span>" specifically. This is the aspect of holy eating — elevating and illuminating the letters within the food through eating, which is the completeness of <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span>, as explained in the Torah "<span class="key-term">Tefillah LaChavakuk</span>." Therefore the Torah forbids eating forbidden things — for they are the aspect of the evil of the 70 languages, where the letters cannot illuminate and sparkle. Only the permitted things, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Targum</span>, are rectified and their letters illuminate through eating — elevating the good within them. Therefore <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> too must be from a permitted species. And see in "<span class="key-term">Tefillah LaChavakuk</span>" regarding the completeness of <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span> through <span class="key-term">Leshon Targum</span>, where it is explained that through meriting the completeness of <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span>, one merits that his eating and pleasures come only from the sparkling of the letters within the food — the essential rectification of eating in holiness.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2>Halacha 2</h2>
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<p class="no-indent" style="font-style:italic; margin-bottom:16px;">The mitzvah of Tefillin according to the teaching "<span class="key-term">V'atem tih'yu li mamleches Kohanim v'goy kadosh</span>" <span class="source">(Siman 34)</span>, see there the entire teaching.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 1 =============== -->
<h3>§ 1</h3>

<p>The essential principle: through the holy <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Nekudah</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[point]</span> — the light of holy love that dwells in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span>/<span class="key-term">Yesod</span>, which is revealed and illuminates through the mouth, "<span class="transliteration">Pi yedaber Chochmos</span>" — "My mouth shall speak wisdoms" — through this it illuminates to the fallen loves that come from the shattering of the vessels of <span class="key-term">Chesed</span>, which fell into the heart and break the heart. Through the light of this <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, they become bound and return to holiness, and all the fallen loves are nullified.</p>

<p>This <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> must be received in three aspects (<span class="zohar-quote">minei ubei</span>): by speaking between oneself and one's Creator; by receiving from one's fellow — "<span class="transliteration">umkablin dein min dein</span>" — for each Jew has a good point that his fellow does not, making him a <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> relative to his fellow, and one must receive from that point; and all these points must receive from the comprehensive <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> that resides with the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span>, the root-soul of Yisrael. The <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Melupum</span> <span class="bracket-def">[cholam vowel]</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yesod</span> — "<span class="transliteration">Harchev picha va'amal'eihu</span>" — "Open your mouth wide and I shall fill it." It is in multiple aspects that are <span class="key-term">Yud-Vav</span> — the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> and the totality of Yisrael, and also "<span class="transliteration">Pi yedaber</span>" and "<span class="transliteration">Hagos libi tevunos</span>" — "My heart's meditation is understanding" — also <span class="key-term">Yud-Vav</span>, and likewise the totality of Torah, see there.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 2 =============== -->
<h3>§ 2</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, whose <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> is <span class="bold-concept">opposite the heart</span> — to subjugate desires and thoughts of the heart to His service. That is, to nullify all the fallen and broken loves through the light of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, which is the light of the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> that dwells at <span class="key-term">Bris Shalom</span>. <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are called an "<span class="key-term">Os</span>" <span class="bracket-def">[sign]</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Uk'shartam l'os al yadecha</span>" <span class="source">(Devarim 6:8)</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Bris</span> where the light of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> resides. This is the binding of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — literally binding: one binds the <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> (the light of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>) to the left arm, which is opposite the heart. The left is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> where the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achara</span> has its hold — the disgraces and broken loves dwelling on the heart. Their shattering was due to the excess <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> within them. By binding them to the light of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, they are rectified.</p>

<p>Therefore the binding is with the <span class="bold-concept">right hand</span> — where the light of holy love is rooted. Our Sages gave primary importance to the right hand because it ties <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> <span class="source">(Berachos 62a)</span>. The essential <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are in the aspect of the right — <span class="key-term">Chesed</span>, the light of holy love dwelling at the <span class="key-term">Bris</span>. The <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are produced through the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> that rises in the light of day through engaging in Torah and prayer at night, as brought in the Kavanos. Our Sages said <span class="source">(Chagigah 12b)</span>: "Whoever engages in Torah at night, a thread of <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> is drawn upon him by day" — this <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> is itself the light of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, through which <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are made.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Kesher</span> of the hand</span> — which is a <span class="key-term">Yud</span>, the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yud</span>. This <span class="key-term">Yud</span> emerges through the illumination of <span class="key-term">Yesod</span> where the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light resides, and it is closest to the heart. The essential illumination of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> to the heart is only through this <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — the <span class="key-term">Kesher</span> of the <span class="key-term">Yud</span>. In truth, the lights of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are exceedingly great, tremendous <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. But from them the light chains down and contracts from higher intellect to lower intellect through many levels, until the light of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> contracts into a <span class="key-term">Yud</span> — the <span class="key-term">Kesher</span> of the hand that illuminates the heart.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">blackness of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span></span> — the "black point." The essential rectification of the shattered is through the contraction of the light, for their shattering was due to excess light. The <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, the <span class="key-term">Os</span>/<span class="key-term">Yesod</span> — the light that rectifies the shattered vessels — initially emerge from the forehead where the rectifying light emerges (as is known), through the aspect of <span class="key-term">se'aros</span> <span class="bracket-def">[hairs]</span> — contractions of light. Therefore they are black, for blackness is the contraction of light — black hairs — the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, the lower intellect, the "black point" that illuminates through the brain's illumination. Then the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates in the aspect of speech — "<span class="transliteration">Pi yedaber Chochmos</span>" — and illuminates the heart. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 3 =============== -->
<h3>§ 3</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> being from the hide of <span class="bold-concept">domesticated animal, wild animal, and bird</span>. The fallen loves are the aspect of animalistic loves — for animals also have these desires. Therefore <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are made specifically from them — elevating and sanctifying them with the holiness of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. The processing requires <span class="key-term">lishmah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[for the sake of the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span>]</span> — one must say with his mouth that it is for the holiness of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. Through this, the light of the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates to the fallen loves (the animalistic aspect, the hide), and they return to holiness — from animal hide, <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are made, which are holy.</p>

<p>The essential sanctity comes through the <span class="bold-concept">writing</span>, for the written letters are the aspect of the spoken letters through which the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light is revealed — "<span class="transliteration">Pi yedaber</span>." <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are in the aspect of <span class="zohar-quote">uvda</span> <span class="bracket-def">[action]</span>, and there one receives the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> through action — through the written letters — just as one receives the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light through the spoken letters of Torah, prayer, and <span class="key-term">yiras Shamayim</span>. The writing on the parchment is itself the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminating to that which is below it — like the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span>/<span class="key-term">Rebbe</span> illuminating to his student's heart. As stated in our Rebbe's words <span class="source">(Siman 192)</span>: the writing of ink on parchment — black fire upon white fire — is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Rav</span> and <span class="key-term">Talmid</span>: what is "blackness" (the lower level) relative to the <span class="key-term">Rav</span> (the upper level) is "whiteness" (the beginning of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>) relative to the second level below it.</p>

<p>The <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> in general are the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>, and they must be enclosed in the <span class="key-term">batim</span> to receive their light through many contractions. From there one receives the light of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yud</span>. This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">hairs</span> that protrude from the <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — showing that the light of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> only emerges through hairs (contractions). Just as the initial light of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> from its source emerges through hairs, so too we can only receive its illumination through hairs — contraction of light — which is the essential rectification. This is also the aspect of wrapping the <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> in hair.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 4 =============== -->
<h3>§ 4</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> <span class="bold-concept">shel Rosh and shel Yad</span> — the three types of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>: shel Rosh, the <span class="key-term">Kesher</span> of the <span class="key-term">Dalet</span> (also a type of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, as explained in the Kavanos of the Arizal), and shel Yad — corresponding to the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>: the comprehensive <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span>; the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> that all of Yisrael receive from each other (<span class="zohar-quote">umkablin dein min dein</span>); and the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> each one receives <span class="zohar-quote">minei ubei</span> <span class="bracket-def">[from himself and his Creator]</span>.</p>

<p><span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> shel Rosh is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> — whose <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> is the "head" and "<span class="key-term">Mochin</span>," from which all receive. He is called "<span class="transliteration">Rosh Bnei Yisrael</span>" — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Rebbe</span>. The <span class="key-term">Kesher</span> of the <span class="key-term">Dalet</span> is the illumination that emerges from there — the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, the totality of Yisrael who bind together and illuminate each other. This <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates through a "<span class="key-term">Kesher</span>" (bond), for its essential illumination is through Yisrael binding together and illuminating each other. This is the aspect of the four camps of Yisrael. The essential element of the <span class="key-term">Dalet</span> is the <span class="key-term">Yud</span> — its <span class="key-term">kotz</span> <span class="bracket-def">[point]</span> — the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>. The <span class="key-term">Dalet</span> comprises a <span class="key-term">Yud</span> and two <span class="key-term">Vavin</span> — this second <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates in two directions, for each one must illuminate his own heart (<span class="key-term">Yud-Vav</span>) and also illuminate his fellow's heart (another <span class="key-term">Vav</span>).</p>

<p>But the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span> (the aspect of shel Rosh) illuminates in the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Shin</span></span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> with three heads — corresponding to the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>, for the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span> encompasses all three. Afterward comes the illumination of the third <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> that each one receives <span class="zohar-quote">minei ubei</span> — this is <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> shel Yad, from which the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates as a <span class="key-term">Yud</span> (the <span class="key-term">Kesher</span> of the hand), illuminating to his heart. The totality of these three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span> is the Name <span class="key-term">Shin-Dalet-Yud</span> (Shakai) — for this Name resides in <span class="key-term">Yesod</span> where the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> dwells.</p>

<p>The illumination chains down from one to the next: the upper <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> (head) — the connecting <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> (middle) — the lower <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> (hand). In every level there are three: upper <span class="key-term">Chochmah</span> and lower <span class="key-term">Chochmah</span> — "<span class="transliteration">Pi yedaber Chochmos</span>" — two wisdoms. The <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> (<span class="key-term">Yud</span>) comprises two <span class="key-term">Yuddin</span> — upper and lower — and the drawing of illumination between them is the middle <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> (a <span class="key-term">Vav</span>). This is: head, middle, end — the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>. The illumination from the upper to the lower <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — from the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span> to each individual — is accomplished through the totality of Yisrael binding together and illuminating each other. In truth, each individual is far from the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span> and cannot receive from him except through the totality of Yisrael — through which the illumination is drawn, enabling each individual to receive too. This is the great advantage of many Jews being present with the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> — the essential reception from the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> who expounds "<span class="transliteration">ba'rabim</span>" (publicly), specifically — for through the multitude they have the power to receive his great illumination, enabling each individual to receive.</p>

<p>This is the three types of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>: shel Rosh and shel Yad — upper and lower <span class="key-term">Yud</span>. Between them the illumination is drawn through the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">retzuos</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[straps]</span> of the head, which draw the light from shel Rosh to shel Yad (as explained in the Kavanos). They contain the <span class="key-term">Kesher</span> of the head — the binding and inclusion of Yisrael who illuminate each other, through which the illumination flows from the upper <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> to the lower. The essential <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are only two (shel Rosh and shel Yad) — for the essential <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span> are only two (upper and lower <span class="key-term">Yuddin</span>). The middle <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> is only the drawing of light from one to the other, hence it has no <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> (<span class="key-term">Mochin</span>). For receiving from one's fellow is only an illumination — even from one lower in level, one must receive from his <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>; and one can receive even without the fellow intending to illuminate — just from the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> stirring from where it resides. But the essential reception of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light as <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> is through the two main <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>: receiving from the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span> (head/brain of the generation) and receiving <span class="zohar-quote">minei ubei</span> when one focuses his mind, stirs his brain, and speaks with arousal — then the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates in him completely.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 5 =============== -->
<h3>§ 5</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Shin</span> of three heads and <span class="key-term">Shin</span> of four heads</span>. The <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> is comprised of two <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>. Also, the comprehensive <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span> (which encompasses all three) is itself comprised of two — for certainly this <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> too has a higher source above it. Even the comprehensive <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> is only the "lower <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>" relative to the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> — "Would that we could receive even from that!" But from the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span>'s own brain — the upper <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — it is impossible to receive. Thus the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> has four <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>: his upper <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, and his lower <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> comprising the three that illuminate to Yisrael. This is the <span class="key-term">Shin</span> of four heads relative to the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> himself, and the <span class="key-term">Shin</span> of three heads relative to Yisrael who receive the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 6 =============== -->
<h3>§ 6</h3>

<p>Therefore <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> shel Rosh are <span class="bold-concept">revealed</span> and <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> shel Yad are <span class="bold-concept">concealed</span> beneath the clothing. For shel Rosh is the aspect of speaking with the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik</span> whose words are all in the open — he expounds publicly. But shel Yad is the aspect of speaking between oneself and one's Creator — which is done in secret and modesty.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 7 =============== -->
<h3>§ 7</h3>

<p>The totality of the two <span class="key-term">Shins</span> is <span class="bold-concept">seven heads</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yesod</span>, which is <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, the seventh day. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Melupum</span> <span class="bracket-def">[cholam]</span> where the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> resides — a <span class="key-term">Yud</span> upon a <span class="key-term">Vav</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Zayin</span> <span class="bracket-def">[7]</span> — "<span class="transliteration">Zachor es yom haShabbos</span>." Therefore <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> is not a time for <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> <span class="source">(Menachos 36b)</span>, for <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> and <span class="key-term">Yom Tov</span> are themselves an "<span class="key-term">Os</span>" — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Bris</span> where the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> resides. On <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> and <span class="key-term">Yom Tov</span>, the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates by itself due to the inherent holiness of these days. This is the essential holiness of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> — the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>. Therefore on <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> all the worlds ascend to holiness, accomplished through the illumination of the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — all the shattered bind to the root of their <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> and are rectified. This is <span class="key-term">Oneg Shabbos</span> — the fallen loves are nullified, and one merits holy delight: "<span class="transliteration">Az tis'anag al Hashem</span>" — "Then you shall delight in Hashem." Therefore <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> is not a time for <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">three meals of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span></span> — corresponding to the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 8 =============== -->
<h3>§ 8</h3>

<p>Therefore one must conduct <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> with holiness — it is forbidden to enter a bathroom with them <span class="source">(Shulchan Aruch 37:3)</span>, forbidden to treat them lightly, and one requires a <span class="bold-concept">clean body</span> — not to pass gas while wearing them <span class="source">(ibid. 37:5)</span>. For <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are the light of the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, and <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> is "<span class="zohar-quote">kevi'a v'kayma</span>" — fixed and standing in its holiness always, never changing, never going out. Therefore one may not go out with them to unclean places — they are the light of the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> itself, fixed in holiness forever, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> — rest and fixedness — "<span class="transliteration">Al yeitzei ish mimkomo bayom hashvi'i</span>" — "Let no man leave his place on the seventh day" <span class="source">(Shemos 16:29)</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore <span class="bold-concept">nighttime is not a time for <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span></span>. The <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> dwelling at <span class="key-term">Bris</span>, revealed as <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, does not illuminate outward except through <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>/speech — "<span class="transliteration">Pi yedaber Chochmos</span>." At night, <span class="key-term">Malchus dikdushah</span> descends to the lower worlds to perform <span class="key-term">berurim</span> <span class="bracket-def">[clarifications]</span>, hence the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light cannot illuminate then. Only by day, when <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> ascends with all the clarifications to holiness, does the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light illuminate and <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are made — for the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light illuminates only through <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 9 =============== -->
<h3>§ 9</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">three daily prayers</span> — <span class="key-term">Shacharis</span>, <span class="key-term">Minchah</span>, <span class="key-term">Arvis</span> — corresponding to the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>, for the <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span> illuminate through prayer: "<span class="transliteration">Pi yedaber</span>." <span class="key-term">Shacharis</span> and <span class="key-term">Arvis</span> are the upper and lower <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span> — day and night, shel Rosh and shel Yad. <span class="key-term">Minchah</span> is the middle <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — the <span class="key-term">Pesach</span> <span class="bracket-def">[opening]</span> that draws the illumination from upper to lower.</p>

<p>Therefore <span class="key-term">Krias Shema</span> is said only at <span class="key-term">Shacharis</span> and <span class="key-term">Arvis</span> — for the essential <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span> are only the upper and lower (shel Rosh and shel Yad), which contain <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span>/<span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. <span class="key-term">Minchah</span> is only the drawing of illumination — the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Kesher</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> (middle <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>), which has no <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> — hence no <span class="key-term">Krias Shema</span> at <span class="key-term">Minchah</span>.</p>

<p><span class="key-term">Minchah</span> is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Kesher</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Leah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Dinim</span>. Yitzchak established <span class="key-term">Minchah</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Dinim</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Vatichheina einav meir'os</span>" — "His eyes dimmed" — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Leah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">V'einei Leah rakos</span>" — "Leah's eyes were soft." The severity of judgment at <span class="key-term">Minchah</span> is the aspect of Eisav's hold — "<span class="transliteration">Vayishlach l'Eisav minchah</span>." <span class="key-term">Leah</span> contained something of the harsh judgment of Eisav — our Sages said <span class="source">(Bereishis Rabbah 70)</span>: <span class="key-term">Leah</span> was destined for Eisav but through her weeping was saved from him.</p>

<p>Therefore all the arrangements of prayer are at <span class="key-term">Shacharis</span> — the upper <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, which is the main one, containing all three. Avraham established <span class="key-term">Shacharis</span>, and Avraham is the light of the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — <span class="key-term">Chesed</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Atah Kohen l'olam</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 110:4)</span>. Therefore the main prayer arrangements are at <span class="key-term">Shacharis</span>, when one prays with <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> at length — the essential <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates at <span class="key-term">Shacharis</span>, the aspect of "<span class="transliteration">Chesed l'Avraham</span>" — the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> being the light of <span class="key-term">Chesed</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 10 =============== -->
<h3>§ 10</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">prayer with a <span class="key-term">tzibbur</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[congregation]</span>. The essential prayer is specifically with a <span class="key-term">tzibbur</span>, for a <span class="key-term">tzibbur</span> is not less than ten — and when there are ten, the light of the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> (the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yud</span> = 10) illuminates. Therefore one then says <span class="key-term">Kedushah</span>, for there is no <span class="key-term">davar shebikdushah</span> with fewer than ten <span class="source">(Megillah 23a)</span> — for the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> is called "<span class="key-term">Kodesh</span>": "<span class="transliteration">V'goy kadosh</span>," and it is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yud</span>. This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">three <span class="key-term">Kedushos</span></span>: "<span class="transliteration">Kadosh Kadosh Kadosh</span>" — corresponding to the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>, each called "<span class="key-term">Kadosh</span>."</p>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Shaliach Tzibbur</span></span>'s repetition. The <span class="key-term">Shaliach Tzibbur</span> is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span>, encompassing the entire congregation, the comprehensive <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> from which all <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span> receive. As stated in our Rebbe's words <span class="source">(I, Siman 282)</span>: the <span class="key-term">Shaliach Tzibbur</span> is comprised of all the good points of the entire congregation. Therefore, first everyone prays quietly, each one individually receiving <span class="zohar-quote">minei ubei</span>; through praying with a <span class="key-term">tzibbur</span> they become included in each other and receive from each other; and then all together receive from the comprehensive <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — the <span class="key-term">Shaliach Tzibbur</span>'s repetition. He repeats the prayer to illuminate to all the <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>. Then, during the repetition, they say <span class="key-term">Kedushah</span> — three times "<span class="transliteration">Kadosh</span>" — the totality of all three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 11 =============== -->
<h3>§ 11</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of the severe prohibition of <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">hotza'as zera l'vatalah</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[wasteful emission]</span>. As explained above, the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> dwelling at <span class="key-term">Bris Shalom</span> is fixed in holiness always, never goes out, and only illuminates below through <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>/speech. Therefore the holy drop that emerges from <span class="key-term">Yesod</span> — the light of the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — is forbidden to be brought out except through the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> (one's wife). Then a tremendous rectification is accomplished — rectifying the shattered vessels, for all holy unions are for clarifying and rectifying the shattered vessels. Therefore the essential <span class="key-term">zivug</span> is on <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, when the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> illuminates.</p>

<p>But when, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>, one brings the drop out in vain — he draws the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light outward, as if destroying the world, giving them sustenance from the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> itself, and renewing the shattering of vessels (as brought in the writings of the Arizal). Not only does he fail to rectify the shattered vessels through the holy <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, but the opposite — he draws the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light outward and the vessels shatter again. Therefore they said it is as if he uproots his <span class="key-term">mazal</span> — for he uproots the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, the root of his vitality. And they said <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> does not avail him — for to where shall he return? He has damaged the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> itself. As long as the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> remains intact, <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> avails; but one who has damaged the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> itself — to where shall he return?</p>

<p>Yet in truth, <span class="bold-concept">nothing stands before <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span></span>. As brought in all the <span class="key-term">sefarim</span>, if he repents, he is certainly forgiven. But his essential <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> is only through the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span> — the comprehensive <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> from which all <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span> receive. Since he has damaged his own <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>, he must receive the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span>'s light anew — which is possible only through the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span>, the root of all the <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>. Similarly understood from our Rebbe's teaching "<span class="key-term">BaChatzotzerus</span>" <span class="source">(Siman 5)</span> on "<span class="transliteration">Ozen shoma'as tochachas chayim</span>" — the essential rectification of this matter is through the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span>.</p>

<p>Since by strict law <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> should not avail, yet nonetheless there is a remedy if he regrets and beseeches Hashem — this is purely gratuitous <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> from Him, accepting in <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> one who is unworthy. And this is itself his rectification: by arousing Hashem's compassion, he evokes an <span class="bold-concept">entirely new <span class="key-term">Chesed</span></span> that never existed before. The <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> from of old cannot atone for him since <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> should not avail; but Hashem in His mercy now renews His <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> to rectify and forgive even this sin. Since a new <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> is necessarily drawn — through this he receives a new <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> (for the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> is <span class="key-term">Chesed</span>), and this is his rectification.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 12 =============== -->
<h3>§ 12</h3>

<p>The essential complete rectification of this is only through <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Mashiach</span></span>, who will come speedily in our days. <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> will come primarily to rectify this sin, as brought in the <span class="key-term">sefarim</span> — "<span class="transliteration">U'nefutzos Yehudah yekabetz</span>" — he will gather all the scattered from the four corners of the earth — the rectification of this matter. <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> is the aspect of the new <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> that rectifies everything: "<span class="transliteration">V'oseh Chesed limshicho</span>" — "And does <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> to His anointed" <span class="source">(Tehillim 18:51)</span>.</p>

<p>"<span class="transliteration">U'va l'Tziyon go'el ul'shavei fesha b'Yaakov</span>" — "A redeemer shall come to Tziyon, and to those who return from transgression in Yaakov" <span class="source">(Yeshayahu 59:20)</span> — "<span class="transliteration">ul'shavei fesha</span>" specifically — those who repent of the sin called "<span class="transliteration">yildei fesha zera shaker</span>" — "children of transgression, false seed" <span class="source">(Yeshayahu 57:4)</span>, as written in the Kavanos that this verse speaks of this rectification. To them specifically the righteous redeemer shall come — for their entire rectification is only through him. When he comes and rectifies them, the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> comprising the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span> will illuminate in them again.</p>

<p>"<span class="transliteration">Va'ani zos brisi osam… ruchi asher aleicha ud'varai asher samti b'ficha</span>" — "This is My covenant with them… My spirit that is upon you and My words that I have placed in your mouth" — the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> dwelling at <span class="key-term">Bris Shalom</span>, where <span class="key-term">Ruach HaKodesh</span> resides, as our Rebbe said; and it is revealed through the mouth — "<span class="transliteration">lo yamushu mippicha umippi zar'acha umippi zera zar'acha</span>" — "They shall not depart from your mouth, your children's mouth, and your grandchildren's mouth" — the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>: head, middle, end — three generations each receiving from the one above. All will illuminate and be revealed through <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span>. "<span class="transliteration">Amar Hashem mei'atah v'ad olam</span>" — "Says Hashem, from now and forever" — for the final redemption is forever. Therefore we then say <span class="key-term">Kedushah d'Sidra</span> — three <span class="key-term">Kedushos</span> — the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> comprising the three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2>Halacha 3</h2>
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<p class="no-indent" style="font-style:italic; margin-bottom:16px;">The matter of Tefillin according to the teaching beginning "<span class="key-term">V'atem tih'yu li mamleches Kohanim</span>," which speaks of the elevation of Yirah <span class="source">(Siman 15)</span>, beginning: "Whoever wishes to taste the light of the Hidden Light (<span class="key-term">Or HaGanuz</span>) must elevate the <span class="key-term">Yirah</span> to its root in <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>. How is it elevated? Through judgment — judging oneself." When one merits <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>, one merits the revealed Torah (<span class="key-term">Torah Shebinigleh</span>), the aspect of Sinai. Through the revealed Torah one merits <span class="key-term">Tefillah</span>, "<span class="transliteration">Chesed l'Avraham</span>." Through prayer with <span class="key-term">mesiras nefesh</span>, one merits the hidden Torah (<span class="key-term">Torah Shebenistar</span>), the <span class="key-term">Or HaGanuz</span> from the seven days of Creation. See there well.</p>

<p>All this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. The essential purpose of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is to <span class="bold-concept">elevate the <span class="key-term">Yirah</span> to its root</span>. <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yirah</span>, as our Sages said <span class="source">(Menachos 35b)</span>: "<span class="transliteration">V'ra'u kol amei ha'aretz ki Sheim Hashem nikra aleicha v'yar'u mimeka</span>" — "And all the peoples of the earth shall see that the Name of Hashem is called upon you and they shall fear you — these are <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>." <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are also the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> — to elevate the <span class="key-term">Yirah</span> to its root in <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> shel Yad (<span class="key-term">Yirah</span>, "<span class="transliteration">yad keihah</span>") and shel Rosh (the root of <span class="key-term">Yirah</span> in <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>, head/<span class="key-term">Mochin</span>). The <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> is the aspect of Torah — the <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. <span class="key-term">Krias Shema</span> said with <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is Torah, and through this one merits prayer — hence one prays with <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. Through this one merits the <span class="key-term">Or HaGanuz</span> — the surrounding lights (<span class="key-term">Oros HaMakifin</span>) revealed through <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, the hidden Torah, the <span class="key-term">Or HaGanuz</span>. This is the <span class="key-term">Shin</span> of three heads and <span class="key-term">Shin</span> of four heads — the <span class="key-term">Or HaGanuz</span>, the light of the seven days — the seven heads.</p>

<p>For most of our Rebbe's teachings said in those times were all said regarding <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — so we heard from his holy mouth explicitly. Although every teaching encompasses the entire Torah, nevertheless specifically the intention of many teachings said then was regarding the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<p>The essential light of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is merited through <span class="bold-concept">rising at <span class="key-term">Chatzos</span> and engaging in Torah</span> — through which a thread of <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> is drawn by day <span class="source">(Chagigah 12b)</span> — and through this one merits <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, as understood from the Kavanos. Rising at <span class="key-term">Chatzos</span> is the elevation of <span class="key-term">Yirah</span> to its root through judgment: before <span class="key-term">Chatzos</span> one says <span class="key-term">Vidui</span> <span class="bracket-def">[confession]</span> for sins — judging oneself for all one has done. Afterward one mourns the destruction of the <span class="key-term">Beis HaMikdash</span> — the rectification of judgment. For the destruction of the <span class="key-term">Beis HaMikdash</span> is the blemish of judgment — "<span class="transliteration">Mekom hamishpat shamah haresha</span>" — "The place of justice, there is wickedness" <span class="source">(Koheles 3:16)</span>, as our Sages said <span class="source">(Vayikra Rabbah 4)</span>. The <span class="key-term">Beis HaMikdash</span> encompassed all these aspects: there was the place of <span class="key-term">Yirah</span> — "<span class="transliteration">Yerushalayim — Yirah Shaleim</span>" <span class="source">(Bereishis Rabbah 56)</span> — and also the aspect of <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> <span class="source">(Berachos 33a)</span>: the elevation of <span class="key-term">Yirah</span> to <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> through the judgment performed there in the <span class="key-term">Lishkas HaGazis</span>, where the <span class="key-term">Beis Din HaGadol</span> sat to render judgment below, nullifying the judgment above. There was the place of Torah: "<span class="transliteration">Al pi haTorah asher yorucha</span>" <span class="source">(Devarim 17:11)</span>.</p>

