<div class="footnotes-section">
<h3>Footnotes — Halachos 1–3</h3>

<p class="footnote-item"><sup>1</sup> <span class="key-term">Tikkun HaKlali</span> — the General Rectification. Reb Noson equates it with rectification of the <span class="key-term">Bris</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> (knowledge/awareness), following Likutay Moharan I:29 and I:19.</p>

<p class="footnote-item"><sup>2</sup> The four <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>: <span class="key-term">Chochmah</span>, <span class="key-term">Binah</span>, <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> of <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>, and <span class="key-term">Gevurah</span> of <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>. They correspond to the four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> placed inside the <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<p class="footnote-item"><sup>3</sup> The <span class="key-term">Shin</span> of three heads and <span class="key-term">Shin</span> of four heads on the Tefillin shel Rosh: 3+4 = 7, corresponding to the seven orifices of the head (two eyes, two ears, two nostrils, mouth) and the seven clean days.</p>

<p class="footnote-item"><sup>4</sup> The three <span class="key-term">Nekudos</span>: (1) the comprehensive <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Tzaddik HaDor</span>; (2) the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> Yisrael receive from each other; (3) the <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> each individual receives from himself and his Creator (<span class="zohar-quote">minei ubei</span>). These correspond to Tefillin shel Rosh, the Kesher (Dalet), and Tefillin shel Yad respectively.</p>

<p class="footnote-item"><sup>5</sup> The Name <span class="key-term">Shakai</span> (<span class="heb">שד״י</span>) — <span class="key-term">Shin-Dalet-Yud</span> — is formed on the Tefillin: the <span class="key-term">Shin</span> on the bayis, the <span class="key-term">Dalet</span> in the knot of the head-strap, and the <span class="key-term">Yud</span> in the knot of the hand-strap.</p>

<p class="footnote-item"><sup>6</sup> <span class="key-term">Leshon Targum</span> — the Aramaic tongue. In Kabbalistic thought it corresponds to <span class="key-term">Kelipas Nogah</span>, the intermediate shell containing both good and evil. Elevating its good into <span class="key-term">Leshon HaKodesh</span> completes the Holy Tongue. See Likutay Moharan I:19.</p>

<p class="footnote-item"><sup>7</sup> The "entirely new <span class="key-term">Chesed</span>" — when one has damaged the very <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> of holiness, the ordinary <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> that enables teshuvah is insufficient. Hashem creates a brand-new <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> never before revealed, and through this the person receives a new <span class="key-term">Nekudah</span> — a concept deeply connected to the Rebbe's teaching on the ten types of song (<span class="key-term">Tikkun HaKlali</span>).</p>

<p class="footnote-item"><sup>8</sup> <span class="key-term">Or HaGanuz</span> — the Hidden Light created on the first day, stored away for the righteous in the future. It corresponds to the Torah's concealed dimension, accessed through Tefillin.</p>

